To facilitate accurate marking, your submitted solution must use this template.


To complete each part of the exercise, add your own code to the following files:
part1: nothing to do, this solution is provided (part1.c)
part2: nothing to do, this solution is provided (part2.c)
part3: part3.c
part4: part4.c
part5: part5.c
additional part (optional): your own .c file(s) in the partAdditional folder

Note: part 5 requires reading key and switch values.  You may choose to use the built-in 
drivers (located in /home/root/Linux_Libraries/drivers/) or the KEY_SW driver you wrote for 
lab exercise 4 (please include your driver with your submission, and note that you will need 
to modify the runall.sh script to load your driver), or you may choose to use memory mapped IO.


Please test your solution using the provided runall.sh script:
part1: ./runall.sh 1
part2: ./runall.sh 2
part3: ./runall.sh 3
part4: ./runall.sh 4
part5: ./runall.sh 5
additional part: ./runall.sh additional


ADDITIONAL PART (optional, 1 mark)
Take your part 3, 4 or 5 program and add something new to it.
The partAdditional folder starts empty. This is where your additional part goes.

1. Put your .c file(s) in partAdditional. Any file name is fine.
   Start from a copy of your part 3, 4 or 5 code and change it.

2. Keep only your changed version in this folder.
   Do not also put your original part 3, 4 or 5 code in here.
   Only one program in this folder gets run.

3. You do not need to write a Makefile. The one in the folder finds your files by itself.
   A file with main() is built and run as a program.
   A file with module_init is built and loaded as a kernel module (for example your own KEY/SW driver).
   Use one program, one kernel module, or one of each.

4. Header files go in the include folder, not in partAdditional.
   Include them the same way your other parts do:
       #include "defines.h"

5. Test it with ./runall.sh additional
   This loads your kernel module first (if you have one).
   Then it loads the built in KEY and SW drivers and runs your program.

6. Fill in the ADDITIONAL PART section at the bottom of this README.
   I'll use it to find and test your work, so be specific.


To clean all directories and remove all drivers: ./runall clean


To submit your solution: ./runall.sh submit 
The script generates a .tar.bz2 archive. Please upload this
archive to quercus (only one team member needs to do this).


The script may generate errors, for example:
ERROR: Removing 'timer': No such file or directory
These errors can be ignored.


If you would like to clarify your interpretation of the exercise instructions 
or provide any additional information to the grader, PLEASE TYPE IT HERE:



ADDITIONAL PART (leave blank if you did not do one)
Which part it is based on (3, 4 or 5): part 5

What I added:
I implemented an interactive geometric shape animation based on Part 5. Instead of only displaying moving X objects connected by lines, the program can now display animated rectangles, triangles, and squares. Different shape types can be displayed individually or simultaneously using the switches. Each shape moves diagonally, bounces off the screen boundaries, and has a randomly assigned color. I also added an option to hide the shape outlines while keeping the moving X objects visible. All original Part 5 functionalities are preserved.

How to use it (which KEYs and switches do what, what shows on the terminal):

KEY0: Increase animation speed.
KEY1: Decrease animation speed.
KEY2: Increase the number of shapes of each active type.
KEY3: Decrease the number of shapes of each active type (minimum one per type).

SW0: Display animated rectangles.
SW1: Display animated triangles.
SW2: Display animated squares.
SW3: Display all three shape types simultaneously, overriding SW0–SW2.
SW9: Hide all shape outlines and display only the moving X objects.

SW0–SW2 can be enabled simultaneously to display multiple shape types. Each selected type initially has one shape, and KEY2/KEY3 adjust the number of shapes per active type. For example, enabling SW3 initially displays one rectangle, one triangle, and one square.
When all switches are OFF, the original Part 5 animation is displayed. The terminal also shows the number of frames rendered, animation speed, number of shapes, and selected animation mode. Press Ctrl+C to exit the program.

